HISTORY.MAN	Command line history RSX Manual

Version 1.9	Novemeber 06 1986

This is the docfile for my first attempt at a history facility for CP/M-80
V2.2 and V3.0. It is basically an extension of the CP/M BDOS 10 call. Under
V2.2, HISTORY is a daemon, automatically loading itself just under the CCP,
intercepting BDOS 10 calls. Under V3.0, HISTORY is a true RSX, again BDOS
10 calls are detoured through this code.

HISTORY will remember up to the last 9 BDOS 10 call lines (command lines,
or lines fetched from inside a program). These remembered lines are kept
resident in memory for fast recall and editing. Full command line editing
(a la WordStar) is possible, as well as total or selective command line
recall. HISTORY will also allow mutiple commands per line.

However, a price must be paid. HISTORY is a memory hog. This is due to a
number of factors: a) this is my first attempt at a history facility. The
current version is 1.9, but the main body of the code remains unchanged
from 1.0. b) The code is 8080 assembly. c) The history buffers are fixed
length (128 bytes each), rather than variable length. Thus 1K of the space
is used merely for buffer space!

Basic keyboard layout is:

+--------+--------+--------+--------+
| !      | @      | #      | $      |
| Recall |   ---  |   ---  |   ---  |
+--+-----+--+--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+
   | ^Q     | ^W     | ^E     | ^R     | ^T     | ^Y     | ^U     | ^I     |
   |  ---   |PrevCmd |  ---   |  ---   | MvEol  | DelLin | DelBol |  ---   |
   +--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+
      | ^A     | ^S     | ^D     | ^F     | ^G     | ^H     | ^J     | ^K     |
      | MvLWrd | MvLChr | MvRChr | MvRWrd |DelRChr |DelLChr |DelLWrd | DelEol |
      +--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+--+-----+
         | ^Z     | ^X     | ^C     | ^V     | ^B     | ^N     | ^M     |
         |NextCmd | DelBol | IntChr | Toggle | MvBol  | DelEol | EolChr |
         +--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+--------+

Some functions have 2 characters (primary and secondary) that will perform
the same function. This is both for the added flexibility, and for CP/M+
conformity.

Note that this is the DEFAULT setup. You can use ANY single character code
(preferably a control character) for any of the mentioned functions.

DELETE CONTROL CHARACTER FUNCTIONS:
-----------------------------------

Delete previous character (DelLChr):
Primary:   CTRL/H
Secondary: DELETE (RUBOUT)
This character will delete the character to the left of the cursor, and shifts
the remaining line left one character. If the cursor is at the beginning of
line (BOL), then no action is performed.

Delete next character (DelRChr):
Primary:   CTRL/G
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will delete the character to the right of the cursor (ie: the
character UNDER the cursor), and shifts the remaining line left one character.
If the cursor is at the end of line (EOL), then no action is taken.

Delete previous word (DelLWrd):
Primary:   CTRL/J
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will delete the word to the left of the cursor. If the cursor
is outside a word, then all characters to the left of the cursor, up to the
beginning of the previous word are deleted (including white space). If the
cursor is inside a word, then all the characters of the current word to the
left of the cursor are deleted. If the cursor is at the beginning of line
(BOL), then no action is performed. A word is delimited by white space (blanks
and tabs).

Delete to beginning of line (DelBol):
Primary:   CTRL/U
Seconardy: CTRL/X
This character will delete all the characters to the left of the cursor. The
remaining line is then moved to the beginning of line (ie: left hand justified
to the end of the CCP prompt). The cursor is at the BOL when done. If the
cursor is at the beginning of line, then no active is taken.

Delete to end of line (DelEol):
Primary:   CTRL/K
Seconary:  CTRL/N
This character will delete all the characters to the right of the cursor (in-
cluding the character UNDER the cursor). The cursor position remains unchanged.
If the cursor is at the end of line (EOL), then no action is taken.

Delete entire line (DelLin):
Primary:   CTRL/Y
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will delete the entire line. The cursor is then moved to the
beginning of line (BOL).


MOVE CURSOR CONTROL CHARACTER FUNCTIONS:
----------------------------------------

Move cursor left one character (MvLChr):
Primary:   CTRL/S
Seconary:  -NONE-
The cursor is moved left one character. If the cursor is at the beginning of
line (BOL), then no action is taken

Move cursor right one character (MvRChr):
Primary:   CTRL/D
Secondary: -NONE-
The cursor is moved right one character. If the cursor is at the end of line
(EOL), then no action is taken.

Move cursor left one word (MvLWRd):
Primary:   CTRL/A
Seconary:  -NONE-
The cursor is moved left to the beginning of the previous word. If the cursor
is at the beginning of line (BOL), then no action is taken

Move cursor right one word (MvRWrd):
Primary:   CTRL/F
Secondary: -NONE-
The cursor is moved right to the beginning of the next word. If the cursor is
at the end of line (EOL), then no action is taken.

Move cursor to beginning of line (MvBol):
Primary:   CTRL/B
Secondary: -NONE-
The cursor is moved to the beginning of line (BOL). If the cursor is at the
BOL, then no action is taken.

Move cursor to end of line (MvEol):
Primary:   CTRL/T
Secondary: -NONE-
The cursor is moved to the end of line (EOL). If the cursor is at the EOL,
then no action is taken.


HISTORY PERUSAL CONTROL CHARACTER FUNCTIONS:
--------------------------------------------

Recall previous history command (PrevCmd):
Primary:   CTRL/W
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will recall the previous history command. If more than one
history buffer is available, then repeative pressing of this key will
backup through all history commands. Once all history commands have been
recalled, an empty line is recalled.

Recall next history command (NextCmd):
Primary:   CTRL/Z
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will recall the next history command. Used in conjuction
with the "PrevCmd" character, this character will let the user peruse
back and forth through all available history commands. Once the last
(most current) history command is located, then next time this key is
pressed, an empty line is recalled.


SPECIAL CONTROL CHARACTERS:
---------------------------

Interrupt Character (IntChr):
Primary:   CTRL/C
Secondary: -NONE-
If this character is entered at the beginning of line (BOL), then a warm
boot is performed.

End of line character (EolChr):
Primary:   CTRL/M
Secondary: -NONE-
Entering this character terminates the command line acquistion. All
characters from the beginning of line (BOL) to the first null (CTRL/@)
are returned to the CCP (note that there is NO terminating null if the
returned command line overflowed the buffer. Also, the cursor need not
be at the logical end of line for termination).

Comment character (Comment):
Primary:   ;
Secondary: #
When this character is entered as the first character on the command line,
the entire line is consider to be a comment, and is ignored (ie: not saved
in the history buffers).

Command separator character (ComSep):
Primary:   !
Secondary: ;
This character will separate commands, allowing mulitple commands per line.
Note that these characters can be the same as other (eg: "Comment" or "Recall")
without conflict.

Recall all history commands (Recall):
Primary:   !
Secondary: -NONE-
If this character is entered at the beginning of line (BOL), terminated
by the "EolChr" character (ie: this is NOT a hotkey), then all past history
commands will be recalled, in list style, prefixed with an associated
history number. 

Selective history recall:
Primary:   !<n>
Secondary: -NONE-
If the "Recall" character is suffixed by a history number, then the current
command line is deleted, and the associated history buffer is displayed. The
cursor is placed at the physical end of line (eg: "!3" will recall history
buffer number 3).

Toggle between insert and overstrike mode (Toggle):
Primary:   CTRL/V
Secondary: -NONE-
This character will toggle between insert and ovestrike modes when entering
characters on the current command line.


NOTES:
------
When a control character is entered, it is echoed on the line as two
characters (eg: CTRL/L is echoed "^L"). However, this pair is treated as a
single "character" when moving or deleting.

A word is delimited by spaces, tabs, or nulls.

If the command line buffer overflows, a beep is sounded, and further
characters are thrown away. It is still possible to edit the current
command line to reduce the line length. Current command line length is
determined by CP/M (usually 127 characters).


LIMITATIONS:
------------
The tab character is not recognized at the moment. Also the CP/M CTRL/E
(insert physical newline) and CTRL/R (repaint line) are not implemented,
As well, the list toggle (CTRL/P) is not available.

In addition, for V3.0, the System Control Block is not accessed, so that
needed values to be got or put are not done so (eg: CTRL/H and DELETE both
will delete the previous character, but this may not always be so).


BYE5 COMPATIBILITY:
-------------------
HISTORY is compatible with Jim Lopushinsky's streamed down history
facility (HIST+). This is a CP/M V3.0 only RSX that uses the BDOS 10 code
to edit a command line, thus making a leaner RSX. Thus, under V3.0,
HISTORY is fully compatible with BYE5, as well as Jim Lopushinsky's
programs: HISTCL (clear history buffers) and HIST (the history program
itself). The latter two being included in the library file.


MEMORY NEEDS:
-------------
HISTORY hogs just over 3K. Under V2.2, it is loaded UNDER the CCP, this
means the EFFECTIVE memory loss is more like 5K. It is for you to determine
whether or not the memory loss negates its usefulness. On the plus side,
HISTORY is completely memory resident (no buffer file), so there is NO
speed degradation if you run a floppy system.


IMPROVEMENTS:
-------------
Feel more than free to improve upon the code. As mentioned, I started from a
blank screen and wrote this RSX without benefit of a predecessor. Keep in mind
that the code was written from a 300 baud standpoint; the line is repainted
as little as possible when modified. This is as HISTORY makes an ideal RSX
for an RCPM system, and not all callers have 1200 (or even 2400) bps modems.


